

**Method 1** (as accurate as desired)

If both \(x\) and \(y\) are "small," (point in double-cross hatched region) use Laurent's series in \(z\) directly. If either \(x\) or \(y\) is "large," use Laurent's series on _0x,_ then on _0y_ and finally use an addition formula. (For \({\cal D}^{\prime}\) an alternative is to get \({\cal D}\), then compute the appropriate root of \({\cal D}^{\prime*}\!=\!4\)\({\cal D}^{3}\!-\!g_{i}\)(\({\cal D}\!-\!g_{j}\); see 18.8.)

\(\Delta\!\!\!>\!\!\!0\)

**Method 2** (for \({\cal D}\) or \({\cal D}^{\prime}\) only)

Compute \(e_{t}\)(\(i\)=1,2,3) (if only \(g\), \(g\) is given use **Table 18.1** to get the periods, then get \(e_{t}\) in Table 18.3; if periods are also given, use **Table 18.3** directly). In any case, obtain \(m\)(\(=\)[\(e_{2}\!-\!e_{3}\)]/ [\(e_{1}\!-\!e_{3}\)]), thence Jacobi's functions sn(\(z^{*}|m\)), cn(\(z^{*}|m\)), dn(\(z^{*}|m\)), from 16.4 and 16.21 and \({\cal D}\) or \({\cal D}^{\prime}\) from 18.9.11-18.9.12.

**Method 3** (accuracy limited by Table 4.16 of \(e^{-xz}\) and by the method of getting periods).

Obtain periods, their ratio \(a\), theme \(q\!=\!e^{-u}\) from Table 4.16. Hence get \(\vartheta_{t}\)(0), \(i\)=2,3,4 from truncated series 18.10.21-.23. Compute approximate \(\vartheta\) functions for _z=x_ and for _z=iy_, whence get \({\cal D}^{\prime}\)(_x_), \({\cal D}^{\prime}\)(_x_) and/or \(\xi\)(_x_), \({\cal D}^{\prime}\)(_iy_), \({\cal D}^{\prime}\)(_iy_) and/or \(\xi\)(_iy_), then use an addition formula (if either \(x\) or \(y\) is "small", it is probably easier to use Laurent's series).

